 ## Map Symbols
 
@@ -40,9 +46,12 @@ A DME-only station gives distance but no radial, so the readouts show DME alone
 When a reference VOR is selected, the bottom-right readout area shows a separate VOR box, for example:
 
 ```text
-NAT R-263° / 12.4 NM
+NAT R-263° / 12.4 NM  ✓
+ROP R-118° / 46.2 NM  ⚠
 ```
 
+The first line is VOR 1; a second line, in VOR 2's purple, appears when VOR 2 is tuned. Each line ends in a status mark: **✓** when the point is within that station's published coverage, **⚠** (and the line dimmed) when it is beyond it — the radial is still computed, but it is not one a receiver could hold. Hover a line for the reason, e.g. *This point is 46.2 NM out — beyond the published 40 NM coverage.* A station that publishes no coverage gets no mark. The marks sit in one column and the box keeps one width, so it does not shift as the distance gains a digit.
+
 This is separate from the coordinate readout so coordinates remain readable. The value follows the mouse position; when the mouse is not over the map, it follows the map centre.
 
 It is the **current** VOR: the chosen reference while the point is within 200 NM of it (`vorReadoutMaxNm`), otherwise the nearest VOR within that range, otherwise nothing. A radial from Ben Gurion over Milan is a number no one can fly; the ident in the box says which station is speaking. On a phone the box sits **above** the coordinates.
